      "role": "user",
      "content": "can u help me compare the thermal dissipation rates of ceramic vs semi-metallic brake pads? looking at high-speed deceleration scenarios specifically. also interested in how heat soak affects the friction coefficient over repeated cycles.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic brake pads typically have higher thermal dissipation rates compared to semi-metallic ones due to their better heat resistance and ability to handle high-speed deceleration scenarios more effectively. Semi-metallic pads, on the other hand, can produce more friction but tend to heat up and wear out quicker in such conditions.\n\n\nWhen considering heat soak—an effect where brake components get suddenly hot due to brake use and ambient or track temperature—both types of pads are reduced in performance, but ceramic pads tend to maintain more consistent friction coefficients over repeated cycles. Silicon carbide or carbon-ceramic materials allow ceramic pads to withstand these conditions better than semi-metallic pads, which may suffer from decreased friction and higher chances of fade due to heat build-up. As a result, for high-speed and repeated braking situations, ceramic brake pads typically offer superior performance.",
      "source": "target",
