      "content": "The erratic behavior you're observing with semi-metallic brake pads during extreme heat is primarily due to the changing microstructure of the material as it heats up. Metal's microstructure changes with temperature: its crystalline structure changes as it warms, which can cause it to expand differently across the brake pad. This differential expansion results in uneven contact with the rotor, leading to a non-linear change in the friction coefficient. Additionally, the metallic content in semi-metallic pads oxidizes at high temperatures, further changing the friction characteristics unpredictably. Once oxidation starts, the buildup of metal oxides can increase pad drag and reduce its ability to dissipate heat until obstructions are cleared by frequent cooling-off periods inherent in repeated brake application.",
